zl1 -- the SECOND heat cause (cpufreq governor), SECOND device run, 2026-09-25
=============================================================================

One boot throughout: boot_id 2fbf9f8e-deea-4955-ad0c-6bddf0fb14f5 -- the same boot as the first run
(docs 170) and the same boot as docs 168. Read again after this run: unchanged. Nothing was flashed,
no partition was written, no block device was opened, no service was restarted, the container was
never stopped.

THIS RUN IS THE ONE WHOSE VERDICT IS AN ARTIFACT (docs 172). It printed COST-MEASURED 3.5 C on a
phone that FELL 7.7 C across the run, and the three defects that allowed it are all visible in this
file, in its own numbers:
  * the two HOTTEST zones (thermal_zone12 and thermal_zone11) fell in EVERY window -- 49.6 -> 47.0 ->
    41.9 and 48.3 -> 46.7 -> 41.9 -- while 21 cooler zones rose by 0.3 to 3.5 C from A to B. The
    phone was cooling hard (it had just finished a heavy load); the governor's own heat could not
    overcome that on the hottest sensors, and the headline "largest warming" is therefore the largest
    of 21 small positives, not the effect being priced.
  * the WAIT accepted "45.4 C" as the reading coming back to window A's 49.6 C. Its test was
    one-sided (`sample - A <= MARGIN`), and 45.4 is 4.2 C BELOW window A, not near it.
  * the CONTROL window was read anyway, and its hottest zones are 6.4 to 7.7 C BELOW window A -- while
    the number the verdict reports for it (C-A = 0.3 C) is the maximum over zones, taken after the
    hottest zone had changed hands. A control window that is 7.7 C away from the state it is a control
    for is not a control, and the run's own table says so in a column nobody's eye lands on.

Host: this laptop.  Device: root@10.15.19.82 (rndis0), serial 33e80afe.  THE DEVICE CLOCK IS WRONG
-- it reads 1970-02-13 -- so every timestamp the instrument prints is wrong and the real date is the
one in this header (the run's own header says 1970-02-13T20:07:39Z).

The only thing this experiment writes is four cpufreq files. The value it writes back is the fix's
own (`interactive`), and after the run every core read `interactive` again.

