== the verdict
   window A was the fix installed ('interactive'); B was the cores PINNED ('performance'); C was 'interactive'
   again. Busy: A 0.81, B 0.54, C 0.82 of 4 cores (a READING, not a gate: a governor changes
   the clock a core runs at, and this figure counts busy TIME, so the two are not expected to track).
   the largest warming from A to B on any tsens zone: 3.5 C  (thermal_zone9 (tsens_tz_sensor8))
   and from A to C (the control, same state as A):   0.3 C  (thermal_zone16 (tsens_tz_sensor15))
   the threshold this experiment calls a cost: 0.2 C -- two steps of the instrument's 0.1 C

   -> COST-MEASURED: pinning all four cores warmed the hottest tsens zone by 3.5 C (thermal_zone9 (tsens_tz_sensor8)), and
      putting the fix back did not leave that warming behind (C is 0.3 C above A). At this load, that
      is what the second heat cause is worth in temperature.

   Read it as a READING and not as the governor fix's price in general: ambient is not controlled, the
   battery's charging state is not controlled, and window C is a control only to the extent that the wait
   above found the reading back (it waited 240s at a 0.5 C bar, and what it measured is
   printed above). The zones are the instrument's numbers, normalised by it; this script does not divide.
   This run used: --seconds 45 --settle 20 --settle-back 240 --poll 10
   --margin 0.5.

   The cores read 'interactive' cpu0=interactive cpu1=interactive cpu2=interactive cpu3=interactive -- the state it started in.

   the scratch directory /tmp/zl1-governor-temp-ab.Wr467O was removed (--keep leaves it instead, and a failed window forces it on).
